=== W6 ~101s drop — leak LOCALIZED to the WiFi TX path (2026-06-20) ===
Instrumented airoc_wifi_host_buffer_get/release with per-direction counters;
gdb-read at pool exhaustion (moderate load):
  pdbg_alloc = {TX 1217, RX 27471}
  pdbg_rel   = {TX 1197, RX 27471}
  => RX perfectly balanced (no leak); TX has 20 buffers allocated-but-not-
     released = the ENTIRE 20-buffer airoc_pool, held in WiFi TX.

But the SPI send path FREES the buffer even on failure:
  whd_bus_spi_send_buffer (whd_bus_spi_protocol.c:289) calls whd_buffer_release
  at line 292 UNCONDITIONALLY (before checking result). So the TX failure path
  in whd_thread.c (whd_send_one_packet line 151-154, no explicit release) does
  NOT leak -- the buffer is freed inside send_buffer.

CONCLUSION: the 20 TX buffers are STUCK in the WHD SDPCM TX queue, not lost.
Under WiFi+BT bus contention the WHD thread cannot drain queued TX (chip TX
flow-control / credit starvation on the shared gSPI bus), so TX buffers pile up
until the pool is empty -> BT backplane allocs fail -> BLE drops. A TX-queue
STALL under sustained coex contention, not a missing free.

Earlier ruled out: airoc_mgmt_send TX-error release (no help), backplane path
(releases via done:), RX path (balanced, per counters).

IMPLICATIONS / NEXT:
  - LIGHT/HALF WiFi load never stalls (held 300s, 0 faults) -> the bounded-load
    W6 gate is achievable as-is; MODERATE load exceeds the shared-3-pin-bus coex
    bandwidth (analogous to the georgerobotics bounded-load certification).
  - A real fix needs WHD TX flow-control work under contention (drain/bound the
    SDPCM TX queue, or reserve pool headroom for the BT backplane), or a larger
    airoc_pool (delays but does not eliminate: 20->48 moved 101s->185s).
